    "seconds": 35,
    "summary": "The anomaly was a bad `targetPort` on the `text-service` Service, which broke post composition. I changed it from 9999 to 9090 and the port now answers.\n\n**What I found**\n- All pods were Running with zero restarts, so the failure wasn't a crash.\n- `compose-post-service` and `nginx-thrift` logged repeated `Connection refused` errors connecting to `text-service:9090`, so every compose-post request failed.\n- `text-service` was up and logging that it had started. Its Service had `port: 9090` but `targetPort: 9999`, while the container listens on 9090. The Endpoints object was routing to 9999, so nothing was listening there.\n- Every other Service maps 9090\u21929090 (media-frontend is the only other non-identity mapping, 8081\u21928080, and it's consistent with its container). text-service was the only outlier.\n\n**What I changed**\n- `kubectl patch svc text-service`: `targetPort` 9999 \u2192 9090. The Endpoints now show port 9090. A test pod connected to `text-service:9090` successfully.\n\n**Verification and caveats**\n- No new \"Connection refused\" errors appeared in compose-post logs after the patch. The last one was at <time>, and the check ran at <time>. I did not send a real compose request, because that would write a post into the namespace. So the end-to-end fix is verified by the port check and the log silence, not by a successful post.\n- The Service is managed by Helm (`meta.helm.sh/release-name: social-network`). A `helm upgrade` or re-sync from the chart could reset `targetPort` to 9999. The fix should also go into the chart values/template.\n- Separately, `user-service`, `user-timeline-service`, and `url-shorten-service` logged failed MongoDB index creation at startup, because their databases weren't ready yet. They retried and started. They're worth a look if you see user- or URL-related failures later.",
